          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a combination code, and which conditions in this reference are coded with one?",
          "answer": "A combination code is a single classification code that holds two related things in one entry. Usually that means a condition together with a complication, or a condition together with its cause. Using one code is more precise than writing two separate codes. Where a combination code exists, the classification uses it instead of the separate codes. (Source: ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions.)\n\nThe reference names two clear families:\n\n- **Type 2 diabetes (E11).** E11 is a family of combination codes. The fourth and later characters name the complication, such as kidney, eye, nerve, circulation, skin, or blood-sugar states. A code ending in 9 is used for diabetes with no complications, which is where E11.9 comes from. Some of these codes need a second code for more detail, such as the stage of kidney disease or the site and depth of a foot ulcer. (Source: Coding type 2 diabetes: E11.)\n- **Hypertension (I10 family).** I10 is used only when hypertension stands alone. When organ involvement is documented, a combination code is preferred instead:\n  - I11 for hypertensive heart disease, with a heart failure code from I50 added when heart failure is present.\n  - I12 for hypertension with chronic kidney disease, with a code for the kidney disease stage added.\n  - I13 for hypertension with both heart and kidney involvement.\n  - I15 for secondary hypertension, coded together with the underlying cause.\n  - I16 for hypertensive urgency and hypertensive emergency. (Source: Coding hypertension: I10.)\n\nThe reference also notes a general rule. When the index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification presumes a relationship unless the record says the two are unrelated. (Source: Coding type 2 diabetes: E11.)\n\nThe reference does not give a full list of every combination code in ICD-10-CM, so this answer covers only the families above. It also does not cover the asthma family, which the reference mentions only in passing. For how these codes apply to a specific record, a clinician or trained medical coder should review the documentation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
